ion only  and so but that means that  once you finish your invitation we're  going to be taking a look at all of the  things that are submitted  including what you provided as well as  the write-up that your interviewer is  going to provide  it is a holistic review process so no  again no one part is necessarily more or  less  important than another but we want to  get a greater sense of  who you are throughout and so  everything you submit is going to be  used both as a way to understand  how we invite folks to the interview  process as well as who we invite into  the class  question here about do we evaluate  career how do we  evaluate career achievement by position  or by promotion  and even though this is more on the work  history side of it  um this might be another thing that  you're thinking about as you are  approaching the impact questions and so  here it's um more about giving the  opportunities  available to you how have you maximized  them  so we don't have a work history  requirement we don't have a work  experience requirement  it's more about given how long you are  or where you are in your career  what are some of the ways that you've  impacted the spaces around you  we know that folks who are applying out  of college or might be very early in  their career  might not have team leadership or  supervisory roles necessarily  but that doesn't mean you're not  displaying some of those  demonstrated leadership um elements uh  that  we find so interesting and so again  given where you are in your career  that's the context that we're  approaching it as  let's see question here about can you  share some of your favorite answers  themes topics that i have seen and so  i think the theme among some of  the essays that i thought really tackled  um  the gsb application kind of  in the full spirit of what we're asking  you to do are the ones that have dug  deep  it's not necessarily that these have had  to be  um either  like very like extremely sad stories  or something that has been out there  some of them have been on very  normal everyday topics but the way those  individuals have reflected on how the  how they relate to those topics and  how that shapes their perspective and  what this means for how they  their goals in the future those have  been some of the most i think  um the ones that have resonated the best  for me specifically  and then for the why stanford again it's  understanding  how the resources at the institution are  going to help you not just  what they are and listing off you know  here are  the classes i want to take but how is  that class  and that topic material or how is that  faculty member's expertise  going to assist you as you go on kind of  into the next steps  of your personal and professional career  so  those are the things the ones that where  people have really centered themselves  in the process and have been open and  honest about it throughout  people who have been able to dig deep  okay um question here from canuck how do  each of the short answers and essays fit  into your evaluation  again here holistic review process they  help us  understand more pieces about both your  context as well as potentially some of  those impact and ripple effects that you  might have had  and so there's folks who have described  it  as admissions as both a science and an  art  and given the flexibility and range that  you're able to cover  we want to be open to that again trying  to do our best to have that holistic  understanding of who you are  um see here we go  essay length okay here's a good question  so do you have any tips on how to cut  down on a long essay  how do you know if an essay is too long  or too short  and so we give guidance on you know the  maximum number of either characters or  words for any of our entries  and so we've found that you know you can  successfully convey your message  in um you know a pretty limited number  of words and i think also as you go on  into your career  i know this is something i struggle with  with emails it's how do you also convey  whatever is meaningful and important  kind of succinctly  and you can also think about this as an  exercise in that  um so if you step away from  the essay and you think about what is it  that i want  to tell them about myself take a look at  the essay you've written  and have you conveyed all of those  points  if you have take a critical eye are  there any like  extraneous elements here that feel like  they might be going off topic  have i strayed anywhere that could be  one place to think about editing  um and i know everybody has a very  different writing style  there is no right or wrong writing style  so  especially for the what matters most to  you essay some people have approached it  with a more  narrative style some have been a little  bit more  precise and organized again  you know how you write is also a piece  of who you are and so i don't want you  to  edit your written voice out  um but as you take a look on  that essay have you conveyed what you  want us to learn about you  adequately and that can give you a sense  there  so for the what matters most and the why  stanford essays  we give like a a guideline for  how many words for both and it's up to  you to decide between the two  like how you want to um use that the  real estate there  um the time when you think if an essay  is too short  is that is there something that you  haven't told us or  is there an opportunity to add that  detail in that space you just don't want  to let  um kind of real estate that could have  you utilized um to go to waste you don't  have to fill  every word but you also don't want to  have an essay that is like  half or less you know just to think  about it like you could have  there's a lot of room there to to let us  learn about you  okay question this is a good question  here a couple questions about avoiding  redundancy with the essay  should the essay be a chain in which  they can link together  other details or would you recommend  filling out the whole application first  to make sure the essay isn't repeating  information found  elsewhere i think there's multiple  strategies that have been suggested here  some folks might fill it out first go  through and then come back  um others might kind of piece things  together and do a little bit of plotting  to understand like take a look at the  application the different sections  and kind of understand where you want to  share different parts of  who you are with us there's no right or  wrong way here  i think regardless of which direction  you go  before you submit take a look at  everything that you've written i know i  hate rereading  anything i wrote but this is the time i  would really encourage you to take  to invest the time in doing so and as  you're looking through it  you know are you saying the same details  okay so  what are the takeaways are they  overlapping  or do they tell me something different  even if they're about like the same  project or topic  again you want to be able to use the  space thoughtfully and so  the subtext here start early give  yourself  enough time that you can go back  make revisions step back step away from  the computer and just think  or talk to those around you who you feel  safe and comfortable having these  conversations with  time is really the best gift you can  give yourself  so that at the end you feel good about  whatever it is you submitted  all right  okay there's a question here about being  told to write about an industry but not  sure what industry they want to be in  and so i think that's a very valid kind  of  realm to be open with so  it's less about do you happen to have a  specific  job industry title as it is you know  there are other  career goals that you might have that  might require  exploration and maybe part of business  school for you  is the ability to explore and get a  sense for  where within that space you might be  able to thrive the best or achieve  you know whatever it is that you wanted  to achieve in your career  that said at least have a vision know  why you're going to business school  and again as you tie it back to that why  stanford  how is the stanford experience going to  help you in that realm  right we don't want you to come to the  gsb and  there are so many opportunities at the  gsb and so many demands for your time  having that vision is going to be one of  those things that are going to help you  prioritize  to make sure you can maximize your two  years here  and so i think that's the other half of  that question is do you know what you're  going to be doing while you're here  not that you have you have to do those  things but having that north star  is going to be an incredibly powerful  tool  um as you embark on your two years at  stanford  all right maybe we have time for about  one or two more questions  uh let's see  ah here we go from natalie question oh  actually  natalie for your questions about letters  of recommendation i definitely recommend  checking out the ask an ao session this  friday  um we'd be happy to answer that one  their question was about kind of your  reference  letters and how do you talk to them  about like what to include  there's a lot of really great  information on our website as well  um so definitely check there there's  information both for you  as well as kind of some information for  the recommenders that you can actually  take a look at  as well  all right and here we go  any preference on so this is from missy  any preference on how the document is  structured  should we add the question at the top of  the page i believe the guidance was to  include  page numbers and have the essays in one  document any other tips for overall  presentation  and so really good question on the  tactical side i know we've covered a lot  like  the philosophical approach to the essays  and so here as long as it's clean as  long as it's readable  um i think that is the way to go  pretty standard text formatting is  useful  um i think one thing to consider is like  we are reading  a lot of essays and so that readability  is going to be important  um so that really is up to you we leave  that pretty open intentionally  um as long as you separate those two  essays and we have a sense for which  essay is which  i think you're on the right track there  so hopefully  the specific format and formatting is  not something that you have to worry  about  too much in the process all right we  have about  five minutes to go  and so here is a question that i think  is very  that will help us zoom back out towards  you know some of the takeaways that i  hope we're getting today and here's a  question about not having any  specific prior business experience in  business  and this question is from manassa and so  will not having any prior experience in  business management studies  impact have a negative impact on my  application or the admission process  and here i like to the answer is no  um there will be there's no negative  kind of consequence if you have not  studied business before  part of the beauty of the gsb  is that it's not just about business but  it's also about  leadership and it's about when you go  and as you're moving forward with your  career how are you going to  lead in the spaces that you're in  leaders come from all backgrounds  regardless of academic background  professional background  personal backgrounds that's where that  idea of diversity broadly defined  really comes into play and we have no  preference one way or the other  we would love to have the most broadly  diverse class as possible  all right here's one more hopefully a  quick one i know i go kind of long with  some of these  but any tips for approaching the  optional questions  um in parentheses there are questions  about the background perspective  and additional information section so um  for the background perspective and  perspective it we really do  ask very intentionally tell us about a  time in the last two years where  some element of who you are has  influenced the way kind of you you've  had an interaction  um and that is one of those moments that  you're able to step back and check in  with yourself  again to understand the hows and whys of  how you operate in the world  so very open on that side  as for additional information section  i know sometimes in your essays there  might be a circumstance that you're  trying to tell us about  and in the essay you might be focusing  more on the takeaways  but there might be contextual pieces  that are helpful or  maybe there's other elements of your  application that you want to explain to  us  maybe there was a health concern  during college that impacted your  classes or  um some kind of career trajectory that  might not seem intuitive at first but  you just want to give us a little bit  more detail about that so we understand  what was happening  that's where the additional information  come piece comes into play  if you aren't already talking about  these elsewhere  you can use additional information  section to let us know that information  um be succinct bullet points can work  and  it's not a place for another essay that  is not the purpose  and so we definitely don't call it  additional essay section  but any other contextual information  that you think is relevant for us to  know  that's going to be the place to do it  again holistic review process at  stanford  no one piece is more important or  weighted  more or less heavily than another we  really are trying to get a holistic  understanding and understanding of the  whole you  of how you're going to show up in our  classrooms and how you're going to show  up in our community  and so with that we are reaching the top  of the hour thank you so much for  joining us  um for this very first um webinar  on the application and the stanford  essays  so as you go forward i hope you're able  to take the time to reflect  um and really understand the things that  motivate you to move forward  as you start to unpack and untangle some  of the things that  kind of inform the way you move through  the world hopefully that also gives you  some of the tools  to be able to stand with and appreciate  those around you  with so much more depth and nuance and  in doing so  hopefully you'll also be able to come to  appreciate  and value the opportunity that the  diversity  of the world around you has to offer so  as you go forward with the rest of this  week i hope you are able to attend  sessions that you find inspiring and  invigorating  um and as you go on to write your  application or fill out your application  write your essays  feel good about it have fun if it's at  all possible enjoy the process and i  believe in you

